WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.960
And so I look at this problem as a
complex systems problem, like a health

00:00:03.960 --> 00:00:09.480
problem. We have one patient. Patient is
planet Earth.

00:00:09.480 --> 00:00:15.599
Our state of medicine is about 18th,
19th century. And we're in pretty

00:00:15.599 --> 00:00:19.830
critical condition. And so we have a
complex systems problem. And coming from

00:00:19.830 --> 00:00:24.300
IT infrastructure again, you know, what we
need is data on the various layers of

00:00:24.300 --> 00:00:29.970
the system. We need much better
tools than we have for analyzing that

00:00:29.970 --> 00:00:35.280
data and looking at the system in a
variety of different types of ways. The

00:00:35.280 --> 00:00:39.570
good news is we have technologies that
we have not applied to this problem yet

00:00:39.570 --> 00:00:44.450
that exist for other things. So the
disruptions in remote sensing, drones,

00:00:44.450 --> 00:00:49.950
surface aerial space—most of that stuff
has not been applied to this problem at

00:00:49.950 --> 00:00:53.579
all.
And likewise with computing and models

00:00:53.579 --> 00:00:58.320
and analytics. So I worked on a project
with ocean climate risk that was taking

00:00:58.320 --> 00:01:04.260
approaches to studying complex systems
in finance and IT and applying them to

00:01:04.260 --> 00:01:09.900
study ocean climate risk. So one of the
ways I look at this is that the full

00:01:09.900 --> 00:01:13.740
weight and force of what the tech
industry knows how to do has not been

00:01:13.740 --> 00:01:18.259
applied to the problem of understanding
the climate system. That's good news,

00:01:18.259 --> 00:01:23.549
because that's low-hanging fruit. So we
could understand the system a

00:01:23.549 --> 00:01:27.780
lot better and a lot faster. And if we
want to intervene in the system I would

00:01:27.780 --> 00:01:34.619
argue that we have to. And I've talked to
politicians and people on the left

00:01:34.619 --> 00:01:38.850
and the right. And what I tell them is
that if you're interested in intervening

00:01:38.850 --> 00:01:42.270
in the climate system, we have to
understand it much better than we do

00:01:42.270 --> 00:01:45.149
today.
And we have to make investments in the

00:01:45.149 --> 00:01:49.470
scientists, the platforms and the
capabilities that we need to understand

00:01:49.470 --> 00:01:53.430
the Earth's system better. It's a
prerequisite for any intervention that

00:01:53.430 --> 00:02:03.479
we do. So that's part of the whole deal
here. And in order to do that, we have the

00:02:03.479 --> 00:02:08.520
mother of all interdisciplinary problems.
And so I don't know if you can read this

00:02:08.520 --> 00:02:12.960
chart, but the disciplines involved go
from nano scale particle

00:02:12.960 --> 00:02:18.930
generation through, you know, localized
weather systems, all the way up to global

00:02:18.930 --> 00:02:24.300
climate data and information systems,
civil and environmental risk and control

00:02:24.300 --> 00:02:29.250
systems, all the way into human and
societal operations and the way we make

00:02:29.250 --> 00:02:36.810
decisions together as a human population.
So that's a tricky one. And I'm gonna

00:02:36.810 --> 00:02:40.080
argue that we need to do all of the
studying that we need to do in the next

00:02:40.080 --> 00:02:44.930
decade because of the timelines that I
pointed out earlier in the conversation.

00:02:44.930 --> 00:02:50.670
So for me, the question is how can we coordinate an agenda to learn

00:02:50.670 --> 00:02:56.130
what we need to know about possible
options for protecting ourselves from

00:02:56.130 --> 00:03:02.280
heat risk in the context of the kind of
damages that we might be looking at, even

00:03:02.280 --> 00:03:09.090
if they're only possibilities by the
middle of the century. And so to that end,

00:03:09.090 --> 00:03:13.040
there are some different kinds of
exploration that we need to do,

00:03:13.040 --> 00:03:17.850
technologies we need to develop. The
aerosol generation—I'll share with

00:03:17.850 --> 00:03:20.520
you guys and we'll talk a little bit
more about the fact that there really

00:03:20.520 --> 00:03:26.100
aren't research programs today. There are
no formal sources of funding in this

00:03:26.100 --> 00:03:30.680
area. And to my knowledge, the only place
in the world developing

00:03:30.680 --> 00:03:35.730
aerosol technologies to try to
perform these techniques and understand

00:03:35.730 --> 00:03:41.340
them is here in Silicon Valley. And it's
a team that's comprised of a half a dozen

00:03:41.340 --> 00:03:45.600
retired engineers,
some of whom co-invented inkjet printing

00:03:45.600 --> 00:03:50.340
and the optical mouse. And they've been
working on this problem for six years

00:03:50.340 --> 00:03:56.730
for their grandchildren without pay. Now
that work is likely to move into a more

00:03:56.730 --> 00:04:02.460
formalized setting where we can work
more actively on different approaches

00:04:02.460 --> 00:04:06.180
and different ways to scale it up, but
the technology is critical to

00:04:06.180 --> 00:04:09.270
understanding what might be possible and
where we're going to feed into our

00:04:09.270 --> 00:04:14.580
models and analytics. And so, alongside
the technology for dispersion, we need

00:04:14.580 --> 00:04:18.000
lots of improvements to our climate
models and analytics. And we need

00:04:18.000 --> 00:04:22.700
specific models and analytics that look
at these specific types of approaches.

00:04:22.700 --> 00:04:26.800
And I could talk to you a lot about some
of the limitations of

00:04:26.800 --> 00:04:31.210
in our reliance on them to make
decisions about climate. For any of you who

00:04:31.210 --> 00:04:34.960
have worked on complex systems, you know
that a big part of the challenge is

00:04:34.960 --> 00:04:39.669
looking at the systems in different ways.
And there are different ways you poke at

00:04:39.669 --> 00:04:44.669
the system and analyze the system. And in
climate we've been using primarily

00:04:44.669 --> 00:04:51.580
simulations, and simulations are
particularly bad at picking up the kind

00:04:51.580 --> 00:04:58.300
of complex system dynamics, like abrupt
change, and so it's particularly likely

00:04:58.300 --> 00:05:02.349
that we may be underestimating the kind
of changes that we're facing. So we need

00:05:02.349 --> 00:05:05.800
to improve these badly. And we need to
get more systems people using more

00:05:05.800 --> 00:05:12.340
different ways of looking at the system.
And then: experiments. So, once you have

00:05:12.340 --> 00:05:17.440
just a technology and you've done some
modeling work process level studies of

00:05:17.440 --> 00:05:20.680
what happens when you release particles
into the atmosphere,

00:05:20.680 --> 00:05:24.819
how they interact with the cloud, how
they flow out into the stratosphere,

00:05:24.819 --> 00:05:30.009
those are really crucial. At very small
scales they can give you a lot of

00:05:30.009 --> 00:05:34.900
information to feed into models and
estimate what's going to happen. So there

00:05:34.900 --> 00:05:40.090
are a couple of proposed experiments or
experimental programs. This one is a

00:05:40.090 --> 00:05:45.009
picture of the program at Harvard which
is the most well-funded program in the

00:05:45.009 --> 00:05:49.710
world for looking at brightening the
atmosphere. It's headed by a

00:05:49.710 --> 00:05:53.979
well-regarded, well-known scientist named
David Keith. It's an interdisciplinary

00:05:53.979 --> 00:05:57.940
program that's been trying to look at
the societal aspects of doing this as

00:05:57.940 --> 00:06:03.159
well. But they're proposing [an] experiment in
the stratosphere using a balloon where

00:06:03.159 --> 00:06:07.930
they would release a number of different
kinds of materials, only a liter each. So

00:06:07.930 --> 00:06:12.789
they'll be very lucky if they can
measure what comes out. But they're

00:06:12.789 --> 00:06:17.169
looking to determine some of the initial
chemistry, the interactions with those

00:06:17.169 --> 00:06:20.560
different kinds of materials in the
stratosphere, and maybe something about

00:06:20.560 --> 00:06:24.819
the way they disperse. And that would be
the first step in a very long sequence

00:06:24.819 --> 00:06:31.120
of studies both in modeling and in
experiments and in data to try to get a

00:06:31.120 --> 00:06:37.750
grasp on whether this would work, what
material you would want to use, and what

00:06:37.750 --> 00:06:42.939
kind of bounds you could put on the risks
of it. So that program is happening.

00:06:42.939 --> 00:06:46.569
I don't believe they have a
definitive date for that experiment, but

00:06:46.569 --> 00:06:52.240
I think they hope to do it sometime in
the next year. And the University of

00:06:52.240 --> 00:06:59.499
Washington is the home of the program
which is not funded, and so consists of

00:06:59.499 --> 00:07:04.270
researchers working in their voluntary
spare time together with the engineers

00:07:04.270 --> 00:07:10.539
who've been working in their retirement,
but what they hope to do is a sequence

00:07:10.539 --> 00:07:15.460
of steps that will go from generating a
single plume over land, a plume over the

00:07:15.460 --> 00:07:19.990
ocean, and then finally a patch of ocean
and clouds that they would brighten with

00:07:19.990 --> 00:07:25.479
multiple ships and try to measure
changes in the reflectivity. And that

00:07:25.479 --> 00:07:30.279
whole sequence that they would like to
do to measure changes in reflectivity of

00:07:30.279 --> 00:07:33.909
a patch of ocean and sky, which would be
your first experiment on whether you

00:07:33.909 --> 00:07:40.319
can brighten the cloud, they think will
take 8 to 10 years to do that sequence. I

00:07:40.319 --> 00:07:46.689
pressed them on it. "Well, if we have
money we can go faster." Okay.

00:07:46.689 --> 00:07:51.159
So but to give you a sense, both of
these experiments that I described are

00:07:51.159 --> 00:07:57.699
below the level where the EPA would even
engage them for a review. So they don't

00:07:57.699 --> 00:08:03.039
contain physical risks with regard to
the kinds of parameters that we have

00:08:03.039 --> 00:08:07.960
today for putting things into the
atmosphere. But people are worried about

00:08:07.960 --> 00:08:12.939
them for other reasons, like the
precedents that they might set or the

00:08:12.939 --> 00:08:27.649
societal forces they might set into
motion.

